<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Computer-Use Agent Instrumentation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain the task context and determine the next action from a running agent, we first instrument the original agent program to be capable of communicating with the monitoring server. The general structure of the instrumented agent is presented in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 4. Design ‣ AgentSentinel: An End-to-End and Real-Time Security Defense Framework for Computer-Use Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the red-highlighted lines indicate modifications to the original code. At line 1, the agent initializes a AgentSentinel client by establishing a connection with the monitoring server, which enables the server to retrieve essential information such as the agent’s process ID and its dependent files. The initial task input is collected at line 3. For each tool-use action, the client notifies the server of an impending tool invocation. All task context messages—gathered at lines 3, 12, and 19—are transmitted at line 11, but only when a tool-use action is imminent. This notification process is non-blocking, allowing the AgentSentinel server to audit the tool invocation passively and mitigate potential security threats in real time. If the auditor enforces a restriction on the tool use, the corresponding security alerts are appended to the tool’s error results. This is the only instance in which the AgentSentinel client modifies the task context. Overall, the AgentSentinel client introduces minimal intrusion to the original agent architecture, enabling users to integrate it with minimal code refactoring. These helper functions can also be embedded into software development kits (SDKs) provided by popular LLM platforms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Agent-to-Monitor Communication Protocol</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a robust, attack-resistant communication protocol to establish a trustworthy channel between the agent client and the monitoring server. We refer to this as the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent-to-Monitor (A2M) Communication Protocol</span>. The protocol is detailed in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 4.2. Agent-to-Monitor Communication Protocol ‣ 4. Design ‣ AgentSentinel: An End-to-End and Real-Time Security Defense Framework for Computer-Use Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where the communication procedures for both parties are specified independently. Both sides are required to execute their respective procedures without error; any failure will result in a panic response, thereby halting further communication. This strict failure model ensures the protocol operates deterministically and securely. Moreover, the sequence of procedure calls is rigidly defined: 1) The monitor initializes a listening server and waits for incoming connection requests from agent clients. 2) During initialization, the agent attempts to connect to the monitor using a provided server descriptor (e.g., URL). 3) A reliable channel is established via a <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SayHello}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑆𝑎𝑦𝐻𝑒𝑙𝑙𝑜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SayHello}</annotation></semantics></math> handshake protocol. 4) All subsequent data communication is initiated by the agent and concluded with the monitor’s response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Communication is driven by client requests, each consisting of an <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">operand</em> and associated <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data</em>. The A2M protocol currently supports four operand types: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{connect}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑜𝑝</mi><mi>connect</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{connect}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{startPassiveTracing}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑜𝑝</mi><mi>startPassiveTracing</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{startPassiveTracing}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{sendNewToolUse}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑜𝑝</mi><mi>sendNewToolUse</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{sendNewToolUse}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{getEnforcementInfo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑜𝑝</mi><mi>getEnforcementInfo</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{getEnforcementInfo}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The monitor server handles operands in a stateful manner. The initial request must be <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{connect}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑜𝑝</mi><mi>connect</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{connect}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains identifying information about the agent. The <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{startPassiveTracing}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑜𝑝</mi><mi>startPassiveTracing</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{op}_{\mathrm{startPassiveTracing}}</annotation></semantics></math> request, which enables system-level tracing and auditing, is permitted only after the initial connection is established. The remaining two request types are valid only once tracing is active. Importantly, the protocol does not permit any operand that disables tracing once it has been enabled. This design defends against malicious attempts to terminate security monitoring, which would compromise the trust model after task inputs have been provided. By enforcing this unidirectional trust boundary, AgentSentinel ensures continuous protection even in adversarial environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span>A2M Protocol</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Procedure</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{AgentClient}(\mathit{serverDesc},data_{\mathrm{AgentBasicInfo}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙰𝚐𝚎𝚗𝚝𝙲𝚕𝚒𝚎𝚗𝚝</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑠𝑒𝑟𝑣𝑒𝑟𝐷𝑒𝑠𝑐</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>AgentBasicInfo</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{AgentClient}(\mathit{serverDesc},data_{\mathrm{AgentBasicInfo}})</annotation></semantics></math></em>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\leftarrow\mathrm{ConnectAndSayHello}(\mathit{serverDesc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>ConnectAndSayHello</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑠𝑒𝑟𝑣𝑒𝑟𝐷𝑒𝑠𝑐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\leftarrow\mathrm{ConnectAndSayHello}(\mathit{serverDesc})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SendClientRequest(C,op_{\mathrm{connect}},data_{\mathrm{AgentBasicInfo}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SendClientRequest</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>op</mi><mi>connect</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>data</mi><mi>AgentBasicInfo</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SendClientRequest(C,op_{\mathrm{connect}},data_{\mathrm{AgentBasicInfo}})}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.4.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{true}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑡𝑟𝑢𝑒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{true}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="op,data\leftarrow\mathrm{GenerateNewClientRequest}()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>GenerateNewClientRequest</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">op,data\leftarrow\mathrm{GenerateNewClientRequest}()</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
